To find the vector \(\mathbf{v}\) such that \((\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}) \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}\), we first need to compute the powers of matrix \(\mathbf{A}\). The matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is given by \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\). We can calculate the first few powers of \(\mathbf{A}\) and observe any patterns. \[
\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A} \cdot \mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = 3\mathbf{I}
\] \[
\mathbf{A}^4 = (\mathbf{A}^2)^2 = (3\mathbf{I})^2 = 9\mathbf{I}
\] \[
\mathbf{A}^6 = (\mathbf{A}^2)^3 = (3\mathbf{I})^3 = 27\mathbf{I}
\] \[
\mathbf{A}^8 = (\mathbf{A}^2)^4 = (3\mathbf{I})^4 = 81\mathbf{I}
\] Now we can substitute these into the expression: \[
\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I} = 81\mathbf{I} + 27\mathbf{I} + 9\mathbf{I} + 3\mathbf{I} + \mathbf{I} = 121\mathbf{I}
\] So the equation becomes \[
121\mathbf{I} \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
\] Since \(121\mathbf{I} \mathbf{v} = 121\mathbf{v}\), we get \[
121\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
\] Solving for \(\mathbf{v}\), we get \[
\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{11}{121} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{1}{11} \end{pmatrix}
\] The answer is \(\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{1}{11} \end{pmatrix}}\).</think> <answer> \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{1}{11} \end{pmatrix}\) </answer>
